	);
}

/**
 * The articles.
 *
 * @return array<int,array>
 */
function veahealth_blog_articles() {
	return array(
		array(
			'slug' => 'dental-implant-cost-turkey',
			'title' => 'What a dental implant actually costs in Turkey, itemised',
			'excerpt' => 'A line-by-line breakdown of what goes into a Turkish implant quote, what the headline price usually leaves out, and how to compare two quotes that are not describing the same treatment.',
			'dek' => 'Most quotes you will be sent are not comparable with each other. Here is what the line items mean.',
			'cat' => 'Costs and money',
			'read' => 13,
			'cover' => 'blog-dental-implant-cost-turkey',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('An implant is three separately priced parts — fixture, abutment, crown. A quote naming one number has told you about one of them.', 'The fixture brand is the biggest driver of both price and what happens in fifteen years. Get it named in writing before you pay a deposit.', 'Add flights, hotel and the second trip before comparing. For a single implant that can erase the saving; for a full arch it is a rounding error.'),
			'content' => '<p>The number people search for is a single figure. The number that decides what you actually pay is a list, and the two clinics quoting you are almost certainly not listing the same things.</p><p>A dental implant is not one item. It is a titanium fixture placed in the bone, an abutment that connects to it, and a crown on top — three components, often from three different manufacturers, at three different price points. A quote that says “implant: £450” has told you about one of them.</p><h2 id="the-three-parts-and-what-each-one-change">The three parts, and what each one changes</h2><p>The fixture is the screw. Its brand is the single biggest driver of price and the single biggest driver of what happens to you in fifteen years, because a fixture from a company that still exists is a fixture whose replacement parts you can still buy.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Why the price moves</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Fixture</th><td>The titanium screw in the bone</td><td>Brand, surface treatment, and whether spare parts will exist in 15 years</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Abutment</th><td>The connector between fixture and crown</td><td>Stock or custom-milled; custom costs more and fits better</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Crown</th><td>The tooth you can see</td><td>Material — zirconia, e.max, metal-ceramic — and who made it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Premium fixtures — Straumann, Nobel Biocare, Astra — cost the clinic three to five times what a budget Korean or Turkish fixture costs. That difference is real and it is defensible. What is not defensible is a clinic quoting a premium price and placing a budget fixture, which is why the brand should be named in writing before you travel and why you should leave with the implant passport that names it.</p><h2 id="what-the-headline-price-usually-excludes">What the headline price usually excludes</h2><p>Read any quote against this list. Every item on it is something a clinic can legitimately charge for separately, and something a cheap headline figure has usually left out.</p><ul><li>The CBCT scan — the 3D scan that decides whether an implant is possible at all</li><li>Extraction of the tooth being replaced, if it is still there</li><li>Bone graft or sinus lift, which a third of upper-back cases need</li><li>The abutment, quoted separately surprisingly often</li><li>The crown, which on some quotes is a separate line and on others is not there at all</li><li>The temporary tooth you wear for three to six months</li><li>Follow-up appointments and the final fitting trip</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>The test for a quote is not whether it is cheap. It is whether you can tell, from the paper, exactly what you are getting — brand, material, number of visits, and what happens if it fails. A quote that cannot be read that way is not a quote, it is an advertisement.</p></aside><h2 id="why-the-turkish-figure-is-lower">Why the Turkish figure is lower</h2><p>The saving is real and it is not mysterious. Roughly, in order of size: clinical salaries in Turkey are a fraction of UK or German ones; commercial rent in Istanbul is a fraction of central London; the lira has lost value against the pound and the euro, which shifts the exchange rate in your favour before anyone has done any dentistry; and Turkish clinics run at volumes that keep chairs and CBCT scanners occupied all day rather than half of it.</p><p>None of those are about the implant being worse. All of them are about where the building is. That is also why the saving is largest on the most labour-intensive treatments — a full arch, where the difference can be £10,000 — and smallest on a single crown, where it might be £120.</p><h2 id="comparing-two-quotes-properly">Comparing two quotes properly</h2><p>Put them side by side and normalise them before you compare a single number. Ask each clinic for the same five things:</p><ul><li>The fixture brand and model, written down</li><li>The crown material, and who mills it</li><li>Whether grafting is included or would be extra, and how they will know</li><li>The total number of days in Istanbul, across how many trips</li><li>The warranty: what it covers, for how long, and what you would have to pay for</li></ul><p>A clinic that answers all five in writing within a day is telling you something about how it will behave when you have a problem in eighteen months. A clinic that answers with a discount is telling you something too.</p><h2 id="the-trip-is-part-of-the-price">The trip is part of the price</h2><p>Flights, a hotel for five to seven nights, transfers, and the time you take off work are real money and belong in the comparison. For a single implant they can erase the saving entirely. For a full arch they are a rounding error against a five-figure difference. Work out which case you are in before you get attached to the headline number.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>Whatever the arithmetic says, do not book treatment on a price alone. Send your photographs and a recent X-ray, get a written plan, and compare plans. Two plans that differ by £2,000 are usually two different treatments.</p></aside><h2 id="what-the-fixture-brand-actually-buys">What the fixture brand actually buys</h2><p>This is where most of the price difference between two Turkish quotes lives, and it is the least understood part of the bill. All implant fixtures are titanium screws. What separates them is the surface treatment, the volume of published clinical data behind them, and whether the company will still be selling compatible parts in twenty years.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Tier</th><th scope="col">Examples</th><th scope="col">What you are paying for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Premium</th><td>Straumann, Nobel Biocare, Astra Tech</td><td>Decades of published survival data, global parts availability, surfaces with the most evidence behind them</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mid</th><td>MIS, Dentium, Osstem</td><td>Solid clinical records, widely used, parts generally obtainable</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Budget</th><td>Various regional brands</td><td>Works. The risk is availability of components in fifteen years, not failure next year</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The practical consequence is not whether the implant integrates — most modern fixtures integrate reliably. It is what happens when an abutment screw fractures in 2041 and your dentist needs a replacement part. With a premium system, any competent dentist anywhere can order one. With a discontinued regional brand, the options narrow to removing an integrated implant from healthy bone, which is a far bigger procedure than the one that put it there.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>Ask for the fixture brand, model and diameter in writing before you pay a deposit, and ask for the implant passport before you fly home. Those two documents are what make your treatment portable.</p></aside><h2 id="a-worked-example">A worked example</h2><p>One missing upper premolar, no grafting needed, single implant with a zirconia crown. Here is how a complete quote breaks down against a headline figure, using indicative numbers rather than a promise.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col">In a headline quote?</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">CBCT scan</th><td>Sometimes</td><td>Decides whether the plan is possible at all</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Fixture</th><td>Yes — this is usually the headline number</td><td>Brand should be named</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Abutment</th><td>Often not</td><td>Custom fits better than stock and costs more</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Crown</th><td>Often a separate line</td><td>Material and laboratory both matter</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Temporary</th><td>Rarely</td><td>You wear it for three to six months</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Second-trip fitting</th><td>Rarely</td><td>A flight, a hotel and time off work</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two quotes can therefore differ by a factor of two while describing exactly the same clinical outcome, simply because one of them has listed six lines and the other has listed one. That is not dishonesty in most cases; it is an industry convention that happens to favour whoever quotes least.</p><h2 id="why-the-same-clinic-quotes-different-peo">Why the same clinic quotes different people differently</h2><p>Because the treatment differs. Bone volume decides whether grafting is needed; the position in the arch decides whether the sinus is involved; your bite decides the crown material; whether the tooth is still present decides if there is an extraction. A clinic quoting everyone the same number for “an implant” is quoting a component, not a treatment.</p><p>This is also why a price given from photographs should be treated as a range, and why a clinic that says so is being more honest than one that gives you a firm figure before it has seen a scan.</p><h2 id="payment-deposits-and-currency">Payment, deposits and currency</h2><ul><li>Expect to pay a deposit to hold surgical dates. A deposit is normal; a demand for the full sum before you arrive is not.</li><li>Ask which currency the quote is fixed in. A lira-denominated quote can move meaningfully between quotation and treatment.</li><li>Pay the balance by card where you can. Card payments carry chargeback rights that a bank transfer does not.</li><li>Get a receipt itemised the same way as the quote, so the two can be reconciled.</li></ul><h2 id="when-travelling-does-not-make-financial-">When travelling does not make financial sense</h2><p>It is worth saying plainly. For a single crown, or a single implant where you already have a dentist you trust at home, the saving after flights, hotel and two trips can be small enough that the convenience of local treatment wins. The arithmetic turns decisively in favour of travelling when the treatment is large — multiple implants, a full arch, a full-mouth reconstruction — because the saving scales with the clinical time while the flights do not.</p><p>Do the sum with real numbers before you decide, and be willing to conclude that your case is one of the ones better done at home. A coordinator worth dealing with will tell you the same thing.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/immediate-dental-implants/">Immediate Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zirconium-crown/">Zirconium Crown</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'why-dental-treatment-cheaper-turkey',
			'title' => 'Why dental treatment is cheaper in Turkey — the actual economics',
			'excerpt' => 'Not a mystery and not a catch. Where the seventy per cent difference genuinely comes from, and the three places where a low price does mean something is missing.',
			'dek' => 'The saving is structural. But some of it is real cost being removed, and some of it is corners.',
			'cat' => 'Costs and money',
			'read' => 7,
			'cover' => 'blog-why-dental-treatment-cheaper-turkey',
			'credit' => 'Euro coins, money & banking —  (CC0 1.0), via rawpixel',
			'credit_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/6034997/photo-image-background-public-domain-golden',
			'keys' => array('The saving is salaries, rent, the exchange rate and chair utilisation — not cheaper titanium. A Straumann fixture costs a Turkish clinic roughly what it costs a British one.', 'It scales with clinical time, so it is largest on full-arch work and smallest on a single crown.', 'Worry about a price below the Turkish average, not one below the British average. The second is structural; the first means something was removed.'),
			'content' => '<p>Anyone who has priced a crown in London and then in Istanbul has had the same thought: something must be wrong with the cheap one. Usually there is not. Occasionally there is, and it is worth knowing which is which.</p><h2 id="where-the-saving-genuinely-comes-from">Where the saving genuinely comes from</h2><p>Four things, and they account for almost all of it.</p><ul><li>Salaries. A dental nurse in Istanbul is paid a fraction of one in Munich. Dentistry is labour, and labour is most of the cost.</li><li>Rent. A clinic on a good street in Şişli costs a fraction of an equivalent in Zone 1.</li><li>The exchange rate. The lira has fallen a long way against the pound and the euro. That moves the price before anyone picks up an instrument.</li><li>Utilisation. A CBCT scanner costs the same in both countries; a scanner used forty times a week costs less per scan than one used ten times.</li></ul><p>None of that touches the titanium. A Straumann fixture is manufactured in Switzerland and sold at a global price; the clinic in Istanbul pays roughly what the clinic in Manchester pays for it. That is the part of the bill that does not shrink, which is exactly why it is the part to check.</p><h2 id="where-a-low-price-does-mean-something-is">Where a low price does mean something is missing</h2><p>Three places, in the order you are most likely to meet them.</p><ul><li>The fixture. Budget fixtures work. The risk is not that they fail immediately; it is that in twelve years you need a component and the manufacturer no longer exists, and the only remedy is removing an integrated implant from your jaw.</li><li>The time. A full arch done properly is not one appointment. A price that assumes one appointment has priced a different treatment from the one you were reading about.</li><li>The aftercare. Almost every complication happens after you have flown home. A price with no answer to “who do I call, and who pays” has moved that cost onto you and called it a discount.</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>The pattern to watch is a quote that is cheaper than the Turkish average, not one that is cheaper than the British average. The second is structural. The first means somebody has removed something, and it is worth asking what.</p></aside><h2 id="what-the-saving-looks-like-by-treatment">What the saving looks like by treatment</h2><p>It is not a flat percentage. It scales with how much clinical time the treatment takes, because time is what is cheaper.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Treatment</th><th scope="col">Roughly where the saving sits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A single crown</th><td>Real, but small enough that the flights matter</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A single implant with crown</th><td>Substantial; usually worth the trip on its own</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Full arch, one jaw</th><td>Large — five figures in many cases</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Full mouth reconstruction</th><td>Largest, and the case where travelling is most obviously rational</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="the-question-that-actually-matters">The question that actually matters</h2><p>Not “why is it cheap” but “what am I buying”. Get the fixture brand, the crown material, the number of visits and the warranty in writing. If those four match a quote at home and the price does not, the saving is the economics above. If they do not match, you are comparing two different treatments and the price difference is telling you nothing.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'hair-transplant-cost-turkey-what-included',
			'title' => 'Hair transplant prices in Turkey: what the package actually includes',
			'excerpt' => 'Package pricing hides more than it reveals. What to check before you agree a graft number, and why “unlimited grafts” is the phrase that should worry you most.',
			'dek' => 'The number of grafts is the price. Everything else is the packaging.',
			'cat' => 'Costs and money',
			'read' => 12,
			'cover' => 'blog-hair-transplant-cost-turkey-what-included',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Grafts are the unit of price, and the number should come from measured donor density, not from a photograph.', '“Unlimited grafts” is the phrase to walk away from — your donor area is finite and over-harvesting is permanent.', 'Ask who opens the recipient sites and how many patients the surgeon covers that day. That answer matters more than the technique name.'),
			'content' => '<p>Turkish hair transplant pricing is unusual: almost everyone quotes a package rather than a rate. That is convenient, and it is also where the ambiguity lives, because two packages at the same price can contain very different amounts of surgery.</p><h2 id="grafts-are-the-unit-and-the-number-shoul">Grafts are the unit, and the number should be justified</h2><p>A graft is a follicular unit — one to four hairs. The number you need is set by the area being covered and by how much your donor area can safely give up, and it is measured, not guessed. A clinic that gives you a graft number from a photograph and a WhatsApp message has estimated it. A clinic that gives you one after measuring donor density has calculated it.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>“Unlimited grafts” is the phrase to be most careful with. Your donor area is finite and it does not grow back. A clinic promising unlimited extraction is either not going to do it, or is going to over-harvest an area you will need again in ten years — and over-harvesting is visible, permanent, and very difficult to repair.</p></aside><h2 id="what-is-usually-in-the-package">What is usually in the package</h2><ul><li>The procedure itself, for a stated graft number</li><li>Airport transfers, and usually a hotel for two or three nights</li><li>The first wash, done at the clinic the day after</li><li>Medication and aftercare kit</li><li>A translator or coordinator</li></ul><h2 id="what-is-usually-not-and-should-be-asked-">What is usually not, and should be asked about</h2><ul><li>A second session, if the first does not achieve the density discussed</li><li>PRP or other adjuncts, often presented as included and then charged</li><li>Anything beyond the stated graft number, if the surgeon decides on the day you need more</li><li>Extra hotel nights if you are advised not to fly on schedule</li><li>Follow-up at twelve months, which is when the result is actually assessable</li></ul><h2 id="the-three-questions-that-separate-clinic">The three questions that separate clinics</h2><p>Ask these before price:</p><ul><li>Who performs the extraction and the incisions — the surgeon, or technicians? Both happen in Turkey. Neither is automatically wrong. You are entitled to know which you are buying.</li><li>How many procedures does the surgeon do in a day? A clinic running eight simultaneous cases is not giving any of them a surgeon for eight hours.</li><li>What is the plan if I keep losing native hair? Androgenetic loss continues after a transplant. A clinic that has not raised this has not planned past your first year.</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>A transplant redistributes hair; it does not create it. Any clinic that does not say that plainly, before taking a deposit, is managing your expectations badly on purpose.</p></aside><h2 id="comparing-packages-honestly">Comparing packages honestly</h2><p>Normalise to a price per graft, then check what that price per graft buys: which technique, who is holding the instruments, how many hours, and what happens at twelve months if the density is not what you discussed. A package that is cheaper per graft because the surgeon is not in the room is not cheaper, it is different.</p><h2 id="how-graft-numbers-translate-into-price">How graft numbers translate into price</h2><p>Most Turkish clinics quote a package covering a band of grafts rather than a rate. That is fine as long as you know which band you are in and what happens at its edges.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">Typically covers</th><th scope="col">What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Up to 2,000</th><td>Hairline work, temples, small crown areas</td><td>Whether a second session is anticipated</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2,000–3,500</th><td>Front and mid-scalp — the commonest case</td><td>What happens if the surgeon wants more on the day</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3,500–5,000</th><td>Extensive coverage, often two consecutive days</td><td>Whether it is one session or two, and whether both are included</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The band matters because the marginal cost of grafts inside your band is zero and the marginal cost of grafts above it is not. Ask what a graft above the band costs before you are lying on the table being asked to decide.</p><h2 id="who-is-actually-doing-the-surgery">Who is actually doing the surgery</h2><p>In Turkey it is legal and normal for trained technicians to perform parts of a hair transplant under a doctor’s supervision. This is not a scandal, and some of the most experienced pairs of hands in the world doing extraction are technicians. What matters is that you know the division of labour, because it varies enormously between clinics.</p><ul><li>Who marks the hairline? This should be the surgeon, with you, in a mirror.</li><li>Who opens the recipient sites? This is the step that decides whether the result looks grown or planted. Many good clinics have the surgeon do this and nothing else.</li><li>Who does extraction and placement? Frequently technicians, and frequently fine.</li><li>How many patients is the surgeon covering that day? Beyond three or four, "supervision" is a word doing a lot of work.</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>A clinic that answers these four questions plainly is a clinic that has thought about them. A clinic that becomes evasive is telling you the answer is one you would not like.</p></aside><h2 id="what-a-second-session-costs">What a second session costs</h2><p>Plan for the possibility. Between a fifth and a third of patients have a second procedure eventually — sometimes because the first was deliberately conservative, more often because native hair kept receding behind the transplanted area. Ask now what a second session would cost as an existing patient, and get the answer in writing while you still have negotiating position.</p><h2 id="what-is-genuinely-worth-paying-more-for">What is genuinely worth paying more for</h2><ul><li>Surgeon time at the hairline and the incisions</li><li>A realistic graft number based on measured donor density rather than a photograph</li><li>A clinic that will say no — to an aggressive hairline, to a graft count your donor cannot support, or to operating at all if you are too early in your loss pattern</li><li>Twelve-month follow-up, because that is the first point at which the result can honestly be assessed</li></ul><p>None of those show up in a package comparison, and all of them are the difference between a result you are pleased with at forty and one you spend money correcting.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/dhi-hair-transplant/">Dhi Hair Transplant</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/manual-fue/">Manual Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'hidden-costs-dental-tourism',
			'title' => 'The costs dental tourism quotes leave out',
			'excerpt' => 'Nine costs that are real, are foreseeable, and are almost never in the headline figure. Budget for them and the trip stops producing surprises.',
			'dek' => 'None of these are scams. They are just costs that live outside the quote.',
			'cat' => 'Costs and money',
			'read' => 6,
			'cover' => 'blog-hidden-costs-dental-tourism',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Grafting, extractions, the temporary tooth and the second trip are the four costs most often outside the headline figure.', 'Standard travel insurance excludes elective treatment abroad and its complications. You need a specific medical-travel policy, bought before you go.', 'Budget the quote plus flights, plus two extra nights, plus the whole second trip. If it still beats home, the arithmetic works.'),
			'content' => '<p>Most of what people call a hidden cost is not hidden at all. It is simply outside the thing being quoted, and nobody thought to mention it because everybody in the industry already knows. Here is the list, so that you do too.</p><h2 id="clinical-costs-that-sit-outside-the-quot">Clinical costs that sit outside the quote</h2><ul><li>Bone grafting. Around a third of upper-back implant cases need it, and it is decided by a scan, not by a photograph. It adds cost and often adds months.</li><li>Extractions. If the tooth is still in place it has to come out, and that is usually a separate line.</li><li>A temporary tooth. You wear something for three to six months while the implant integrates. Sometimes included; frequently not.</li><li>The second trip. Most implant treatment is two trips, and the second one is a flight, a hotel and time off work that the first quote did not mention.</li></ul><h2 id="travel-costs-people-underestimate">Travel costs people underestimate</h2><ul><li>Extra nights if you are told not to fly on the day you planned to.</li><li>A companion. For a full-arch case under sedation, somebody has to be with you, and that is a second flight and a second bed.</li><li>Time off work. Five to seven days for most implant cases; more if you are doing anything physical.</li></ul><h2 id="what-happens-if-something-goes-wrong-at-">What happens if something goes wrong at home</h2><p>This is the expensive one, and the one nobody prices. If a crown debonds in Leeds eight months later, someone in Leeds has to re-cement it, and your Istanbul warranty does not pay a British dentist. Some clinics have partner arrangements at home; most do not. Ask the question before you travel, and get the answer in writing.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>Standard travel insurance does not cover elective treatment abroad or its complications. If you want cover you need a specific medical-travel policy, bought before you go, and you should read what it excludes rather than what it advertises.</p></aside><h2 id="the-honest-budget">The honest budget</h2><p>Take the quote. Add flights and accommodation for the stated stay, then add two nights. Add the second trip in full. Add a contingency for grafting unless a scan has already ruled it out. If the total still beats your quote at home by a margin that makes the journey worth it, the arithmetic works — and now it is arithmetic rather than hope.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/bone-graft/">Bone Graft</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sinus-lift/">Sinus Lift</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'red-flags-dental-tourism-quote',
			'title' => 'Eleven red flags in a dental tourism quote',
			'excerpt' => 'Signals that reliably separate a clinic that will look after you from one that will take a deposit. None of them require dental knowledge to spot.',
			'dek' => 'You do not need to know dentistry to read a quote. You need to know what is missing from it.',
			'cat' => 'Safety and choosing a clinic',
			'read' => 7,
			'cover' => 'blog-red-flags-dental-tourism-quote',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('No fixture brand, no crown material, and no scan before a fixed price are the three clearest signals in a quote.', 'A discount that appears when you hesitate, and a deposit requested before anything is in writing, are signals about behaviour.', 'Ask what happens if it fails in two years — who fixes it, where, and who pays. How fast a clear answer comes tells you most of what you need.'),
			'content' => '<p>You will not be able to judge the clinical plan. That is fine — you are not supposed to be able to. What you can judge is how a clinic communicates, what it commits to in writing, and what it does when you ask an awkward question. Those correlate with clinical care more strongly than anything on the website.</p><h2 id="in-the-quote-itself">In the quote itself</h2><ul><li>No fixture brand named. The single most important component is unnamed. Ask; if the answer stays vague, that is the answer.</li><li>No crown material named. “Porcelain” is not a material specification.</li><li>One price for everything, with no line items. You cannot compare it, which is often the point.</li><li>A treatment plan produced without a scan. Nobody can plan implants from photographs. They can sell them from photographs.</li><li>No mention of grafting either way. A plan that has not addressed it has not been made by someone looking at your bone.</li></ul><h2 id="in-how-they-behave">In how they behave</h2><ul><li>A discount that appears when you hesitate. Clinical pricing does not work like a carpet shop.</li><li>Pressure to book flights before a plan exists.</li><li>A deposit requested before you have anything in writing.</li><li>Answers only ever on WhatsApp, never on headed paper or email.</li><li>Reluctance to put you in touch with the treating dentist before you travel.</li><li>A guarantee with no document behind it. “Lifetime guarantee” said in a chat is not a guarantee; it is a sentence.</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>The most useful question you can ask is boring: “What happens if this fails in two years — who fixes it, where, and who pays?” The quality of the answer, and how fast it comes, tells you more than any before-and-after gallery.</p></aside><h2 id="things-that-are-not-red-flags">Things that are not red flags</h2><p>Some things alarm people and should not. A very large clinic is not a bad sign; volume is why the price works. Technicians doing parts of a hair transplant is normal and legal in Turkey — what matters is which parts and whether you were told. And a price far below your home country is the whole point of the exercise; it is a price far below the local Turkish average that should make you ask what has been removed.</p><h2 id="one-document-to-ask-for">One document to ask for</h2><p>Ask for the clinic’s health tourism authorisation from the Turkish Ministry of Health. Facilities treating international patients are required to hold one, and a clinic that holds it can send it to you the same day. A clinic that cannot produce it, or does not know what you are asking about, has answered a different and more important question.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zirconium-crown/">Zirconium Crown</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'how-to-check-turkish-clinic-legitimate',
			'title' => 'How to check a Turkish clinic is legitimate, in an afternoon',
			'excerpt' => 'Six checks you can do yourself before you send a deposit — what to ask for, where to verify it, and what a straight answer looks like.',
			'dek' => 'Every one of these can be done from your sofa, and none of them requires you to know any dentistry.',
			'cat' => 'Safety and choosing a clinic',
			'read' => 13,
			'cover' => 'blog-how-to-check-turkish-clinic-legitimate',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Ask for the Ministry of Health tourism authorisation as a file. A clinic that holds it can send it the same day.', 'Insist on a video call with the treating clinician before any deposit, and ask what would make them decline to treat you.', 'Read the three-star reviews, not the five- or one-star ones, and check the address on the certificate matches the building in your itinerary.'),
			'content' => '<p>The uncomfortable truth about choosing a clinic abroad is that the marketing is excellent everywhere. Every website has the same gallery, the same certificates, the same testimonials. What separates clinics is not the website; it is what they will put in writing and how fast.</p><h2 id="1-the-health-tourism-authorisation">1. The health tourism authorisation</h2><p>Turkey regulates this. Facilities treating international patients are required to hold a health tourism authorisation certificate from the Ministry of Health, and the international side of it is coordinated through USHAŞ, the state-owned international health services company. A clinic that holds the certificate can send you a copy the same day. Ask for it in your first message. The speed of the reply is data.</p><h2 id="2-the-dentist-by-name">2. The dentist, by name</h2><p>Ask who will treat you, and ask for their name and registration. Not the clinic’s founder in the About page — the person who will be holding the drill. If a clinic will not tell you before you have paid, consider what else it will not tell you before you have paid.</p><h2 id="3-a-video-call-before-the-deposit">3. A video call before the deposit</h2><p>Ten minutes with the treating dentist, before money changes hands. Clinics that do this routinely are the ones with clinicians who have time. Clinics that route everything through a sales coordinator and promise you will meet the dentist on the day have told you how the day will run.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>Ask on the call: “How many of these do you do a month, and what is your most common complication?” A clinician will answer the second half without hesitating. A salesperson will tell you there are no complications, which is not true of any surgical procedure ever performed.</p></aside><h2 id="4-the-plan-on-paper-from-a-scan">4. The plan, on paper, from a scan</h2><p>A real plan follows a CBCT scan and names components. If you are being given a treatment plan and a fixed price from three phone photographs, you are being sold to, not assessed. Some clinics will quote a range from photographs and say plainly that it is a range pending a scan — that is honest and it is different.</p><h2 id="5-reviews-read-the-right-way">5. Reviews, read the right way</h2><p>Ignore the five-star reviews and ignore the one-star reviews. Read the three-star ones: they are written by people with no agenda, and they are where you find the recurring operational complaints — the transfer that did not arrive, the coordinator who stopped replying after payment. Then check whether the clinic replied, and how.</p><p>Be sceptical of galleries. Before-and-after photographs on a Turkish clinic site are frequently not that clinic’s patients. Ask for a case that matches yours, with dates.</p><h2 id="6-what-happens-afterwards-in-writing">6. What happens afterwards, in writing</h2><ul><li>Who do I contact in the first fortnight, and on what number?</li><li>If a crown debonds at home in eight months, what exactly do I do?</li><li>What does the warranty cover, for how long, and what voids it?</li><li>Will I get my implant passport, X-rays and treatment record to take home?</li></ul><p>That last one matters more than it sounds. Whatever happens later, any dentist anywhere can help you far more easily if you can hand them a document naming the fixture that is in your jaw.</p><h2 id="the-pattern-not-the-answer">The pattern, not the answer</h2><p>No single check is decisive. What is decisive is the pattern: a clinic that answers all six clearly, in writing, within a day or two, is a clinic with systems. A clinic that answers three of them with enthusiasm and the rest with a discount is telling you what it will be like when something goes wrong.</p><h2 id="reading-a-before-and-after-gallery">Reading a before-and-after gallery</h2><p>Galleries are the most persuasive and least reliable thing on any clinic website. A significant share of images on Turkish clinic sites are not that clinic’s patients — they circulate, get reused, and occasionally belong to a practice in another country entirely. Here is how to test one.</p><ul><li>Ask for the dates of both photographs. For hair, anything less than twelve months apart is not a result.</li><li>Compare the lighting, the angle and the camera. Different lighting between before and after is the oldest trick there is.</li><li>For hair, watch for a wet, flattened "before" and a dry, styled "after".</li><li>Ask for a case that matches yours — similar age, similar starting point, similar hair type or bone level.</li><li>Ask directly: are these your own patients, treated in this building, by the clinician who would treat me?</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>You can reverse-image-search a gallery photograph in about thirty seconds. If it appears on four other clinics’ websites, you have learned something important for very little effort.</p></aside><h2 id="what-to-ask-on-the-video-call">What to ask on the video call</h2><p>Ten minutes with the treating clinician is worth more than a week of reading reviews. Use it well.</p><ul><li>How many of these do you do a month?</li><li>What is your most common complication, and how do you handle it?</li><li>What would make you tell me not to have this treatment?</li><li>Who else will be in the room, and what will they be doing?</li><li>What does my scan show about my bone, specifically?</li></ul><p>The third question is the most useful one you will ask. Every genuine clinician has a list of people they turn away. Somebody who cannot name a single circumstance in which they would decline you is not assessing you.</p><h2 id="checking-the-building-not-just-the-brand">Checking the building, not just the brand</h2><p>Turkish health tourism has a layer of intermediaries: agencies that market under one name and place patients in whichever clinic has capacity. That arrangement is not automatically bad, but you should know whether it is what you are buying. Ask for the name and address of the facility you would actually attend, and check that it matches the name on the health tourism authorisation you were sent. If it does not, ask why before you book flights.</p><h2 id="the-two-week-test">The two-week test</h2><p>Before committing, send one non-urgent question by email on a weekday afternoon and see what happens. A clinic that answers clearly within a working day, in writing, from a named person, will behave that way when you message from home at week three with a worry. A clinic that answers in four days with a voice note and a discount code will behave that way too.</p><p>It costs you nothing and it is the single most predictive thing you can do.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'health-tourism-authorisation-turkey',
			'title' => 'What Turkey’s health tourism authorisation actually means',
			'excerpt' => 'The certificate every clinic mentions, what it does and does not guarantee, and the other accreditations worth asking about.',
			'dek' => 'It is a real regulatory requirement. It is also not a quality rating, and clinics use it as though it were.',
			'cat' => 'Safety and choosing a clinic',
			'read' => 6,
			'cover' => 'blog-health-tourism-authorisation-turkey',
			'credit' => 'No Known Restrictions: Public Domain: Mosque at St. Sophia from Bain Collection, no date (LOC) — pingnews.com (PDM 1.0), via flickr',
			'credit_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/39735679@N00/466713875',
			'keys' => array('It is a licence to operate, not a quality ranking. Holding it means the clinic has cleared the floor, not that it is good.', 'A clinic without it is operating outside the rules for treating you, which is a straightforward reason to walk away.', 'JCI accreditation is a hospital standard and is rare in dental clinics — its absence there means very little.'),
			'content' => '<p>Almost every Turkish clinic website mentions being “authorised” or “certified” for health tourism. It is a real thing and it is worth understanding, mostly so you know what it does not tell you.</p><h2 id="what-it-is">What it is</h2><p>Turkey requires facilities that treat international patients to hold a health tourism authorisation issued under Ministry of Health regulation. The international coordination sits with USHAŞ (Uluslararası Sağlık Hizmetleri A.Ş.), the state-owned company set up to organise the sector. Holding the authorisation means the facility has met defined requirements — premises, staffing, interpreter provision, record-keeping — and is permitted to treat patients from abroad.</p><h2 id="what-it-is-not">What it is not</h2><p>It is a licence to operate, not a ranking. It does not say the surgeon is good, it does not say the fixtures are premium, and it does not say your case will go well. Treating it as a quality mark is the mistake the marketing invites you to make.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>A clinic without it is a clinic operating outside the rules for treating you, and that is a straightforward reason to walk away. A clinic with it is a clinic that has cleared the floor — which is where your questions start, not where they finish.</p></aside><h2 id="the-other-things-worth-asking-about">The other things worth asking about</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">What to ask for</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Health tourism authorisation</th><td>The facility is legally permitted to treat international patients</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">JCI accreditation</th><td>International hospital standard. Common in large hospitals, rare in dental clinics — its absence in a dental clinic means little</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ISO 9001</th><td>A quality management system exists. Says nothing clinical, but says somebody writes procedures down</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The treating clinician’s registration</th><td>That the person treating you is a registered dentist or surgeon in Turkey</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sterilisation records</th><td>That the autoclave is validated and logged. Rarely asked for, always available in a good clinic</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="how-to-verify-rather-than-trust">How to verify rather than trust</h2><p>Ask for the certificate as a file, not as a badge on a web page. Check that the name on it is the name of the facility you would actually attend — group brands sometimes market under one name and treat you in a different building. If the address on the certificate and the address in your itinerary are not the same, ask why before you fly.</p><h2 id="if-something-goes-wrong">If something goes wrong</h2><p>Complaints about Turkish facilities go to the provincial health directorate and, for health tourism specifically, through the Ministry’s channels. It is a real process. It is also slow, in Turkish, and not a substitute for choosing carefully in the first place — which is why the boring questions before you travel are worth more than any recourse afterwards.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'all-on-4-vs-all-on-6',
			'title' => 'All-on-4 or All-on-6: how the choice is actually made',
			'excerpt' => 'Not a matter of more being better. What the two extra implants buy you, when they are necessary, and when paying for them is money spent on nothing.',
			'dek' => 'Your bone decides this, not your budget and not a brochure.',
			'cat' => 'Choosing a treatment',
			'read' => 12,
			'cover' => 'blog-all-on-4-vs-all-on-6',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('A CBCT scan decides this, not your budget. Four tilted implants often avoid grafting; six spread the load where the bone allows.', 'More implants is not automatically stronger — four in good bone beat six placed into poor bone.', 'Ask which prosthesis is included at each stage. Acrylic provisional versus definitive zirconia can change a full-arch price by thousands.'),
			'content' => '<p>It is presented as a menu — four implants or six, cheaper or dearer. It is not really a menu. It is a structural decision made from a scan, and the honest answer for most people is that one of the two is indicated and the other is not.</p><h2 id="what-the-numbers-mean">What the numbers mean</h2><p>Both are ways of carrying a full fixed arch on a small number of implants. The four-implant version tilts the two rear fixtures, typically at around thirty degrees, so they engage bone that is further forward and denser — which is precisely how it avoids the sinus and often avoids grafting altogether. The six-implant version spreads the same load across two more supports.</p><h2 id="when-six-is-the-right-answer">When six is the right answer</h2><ul><li>Where there is enough bone volume to place them without grafting, so the extra implants cost you fixtures rather than months</li><li>Heavy bite forces, particularly in patients who grind</li><li>A longer arch, where the cantilever behind the last implant would otherwise be doing too much work</li><li>Where you want redundancy: with six, losing one is a repair; with four, losing one can mean the whole arch comes off</li></ul><h2 id="when-four-is-the-right-answer">When four is the right answer</h2><ul><li>Limited posterior bone, where six would mean a sinus lift and a six-month delay</li><li>Cases where same-day fixed teeth matter — four is the protocol with the most evidence behind immediate loading</li><li>Lower jaws, where bone is denser and four supports are frequently ample</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>More implants is not automatically stronger. Implants placed into poor bone because the plan called for six are weaker than four placed into good bone. Where the fixtures go matters more than how many there are.</p></aside><h2 id="what-actually-decides-it">What actually decides it</h2><p>A CBCT scan showing bone height and density in each site, your bite, the shape of your arch, and whether you grind. That is the whole list. A clinic quoting you one or the other before it has a scan is quoting a price, not proposing a treatment.</p><h2 id="the-cost-difference-in-context">The cost difference in context</h2><p>Two extra fixtures and two extra abutments is a real difference, but it is a small fraction of a full-arch total — the prosthesis, the surgical time and the laboratory work dominate. If six is indicated, the extra is worth paying. If it is not indicated, you are paying for hardware that is doing nothing, and the honest clinic will tell you so.</p><p>The question to ask is not “which is better”. It is: “From my scan, which do you recommend, and what specifically about my bone makes you say that?” The answer should mention your anatomy. If it mentions the price list instead, ask somebody else.</p><h2 id="what-immediate-loading-actually-means">What immediate loading actually means</h2><p>Both protocols are usually described as “teeth in a day”, and both usually are — but the tooth you leave with is a provisional. Immediate loading means fitting a fixed temporary bridge onto the implants at the time of surgery, so you are never without teeth. It works because a rigid bridge splints the implants together and stops any one of them moving while bone grows onto it.</p><p>The requirement is primary stability: the implants must be tight in the bone at the moment they are placed. Where the surgeon cannot achieve it, immediate loading is not safe and the honest answer is a removable temporary for a few months. A clinic that promises fixed same-day teeth before seeing your scan is promising something it cannot yet know.</p><h2 id="the-prosthesis-matters-as-much-as-the-im">The prosthesis matters as much as the implants</h2><p>The conversation is always about four implants or six. What you will actually live with is the bridge on top of them, and that choice has more effect on your daily experience.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Prosthesis</th><th scope="col">Feel and appearance</th><th scope="col">Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Acrylic on a metal frame</th><td>Lighter, cheaper, easier to repair</td><td>Wears and stains over years; needs replacing</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Monolithic zirconia</th><td>Strongest, most stain-resistant, best long-term</td><td>Costs more; a fracture means a remake</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Porcelain-layered zirconia</th><td>The most natural light behaviour</td><td>Layering can chip under heavy bite forces</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Many quotes bundle a provisional acrylic bridge and a definitive zirconia one. Some quote only the provisional and present the definitive as an upgrade. That single difference can change a full-arch price by thousands, and it is not visible unless you ask which prosthesis is included at each stage.</p><h2 id="cantilever-and-why-the-back-teeth-are-th">Cantilever, and why the back teeth are the argument</h2><p>On four implants, the bridge extends behind the rearmost fixture — a cantilever. That overhang is doing unsupported work every time you chew on it, and the longer it is, the more leverage it applies to the implant in front. Well-designed four-implant arches keep it short, typically to one tooth per side. This is the technical reason six implants is preferred in some cases: the extra supports move the load forward and shorten the cantilever.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>If you are told six implants and not why, ask whether it is about bone, about bite force, or about cantilever length. All three are legitimate answers. "More is better" is not one.</p></aside><h2 id="living-with-a-full-arch">Living with a full arch</h2><ul><li>Cleaning underneath is not optional. A full arch traps plaque against gum, and peri-implantitis around a full arch is a serious, expensive problem.</li><li>Expect an adjustment period of weeks for speech and eating, not days.</li><li>The bite gets refined over more than one appointment; that is normal, not a fault.</li><li>Plan for maintenance visits. Screws are checked and retorqued periodically for the life of the prosthesis.</li></ul><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/all-on-4-dental-implants/">All On 4 Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/all-on-6-dental-implants/">All On 6 Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'zirconia-vs-emax-crowns',
			'title' => 'Zirconia or e.max: which crown belongs on which tooth',
			'excerpt' => 'One is stronger, one is prettier, and the right answer changes depending on which tooth you are talking about. A straight comparison.',
			'dek' => 'Most disappointing crown work is the right material in the wrong place.',
			'cat' => 'Choosing a treatment',
			'read' => 6,
			'cover' => 'blog-zirconia-vs-emax-crowns',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Zirconia for molars, bridges, grinders and full arches. e.max for front teeth and veneers, where translucency decides the result.', 'Molars are a fatigue problem, not a one-off load problem, which is why e.max is the wrong choice at the back for anyone who clenches.', 'The ceramist matters more than the material. Ask who makes the crown, not only what it is made of.'),
			'content' => '<p>These are the two materials you will be offered for most crown and veneer work, and they are genuinely different. Neither is better. They are good at different things, and a clinic that offers one for everything is choosing on inventory rather than on your mouth.</p><h2 id="the-short-version">The short version</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Zirconia</th><th scope="col">Lithium disilicate (e.max)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Strength</th><td>Very high — the strongest routine option</td><td>Good, but well below zirconia</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Light behaviour</th><td>Historically more opaque; modern layered zirconia is much better</td><td>Excellent — translucency closest to natural enamel</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Molars, bridges, grinders, full arches</td><td>Front teeth, veneers, single anterior crowns</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Tooth reduction</th><td>Can be minimal</td><td>Can be very minimal for veneers</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Weak point</th><td>In the wrong hands, can look flat at the front</td><td>Can fracture under heavy posterior load</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="why-the-back-of-the-mouth-is-a-strength-">Why the back of the mouth is a strength problem</h2><p>Molars generate the highest bite forces in the body, and they do it thousands of times a day. That is a fatigue problem, not a one-off load problem, and it is why zirconia dominates posteriorly. An e.max crown on a molar in someone who clenches is a crown with a shorter life than it should have.</p><h2 id="why-the-front-of-the-mouth-is-a-light-pr">Why the front of the mouth is a light problem</h2><p>Natural enamel is translucent: light enters it, scatters, and leaves. Matching that is what makes an anterior crown invisible, and it is the thing e.max does best. A front crown that is the right shade but the wrong translucency reads as false even to people who cannot say why.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>The material matters less than the ceramist. A well-layered zirconia crown from a good laboratory beats a badly made e.max crown every time. When you are comparing quotes, ask who makes the crown as well as what it is made of.</p></aside><h2 id="where-it-gets-decided-for-you">Where it gets decided for you</h2><ul><li>Bridges spanning more than one missing tooth: zirconia, for the strength</li><li>A single front tooth next to natural teeth: usually e.max, because matching one tooth to its neighbour is the hardest thing in dentistry</li><li>Full-arch prosthetics: zirconia</li><li>Veneers on intact enamel: e.max, and as little preparation as possible</li><li>Anyone who grinds: zirconia, and a night guard, and the night guard is not optional</li></ul><h2 id="the-question-to-ask">The question to ask</h2><p>Ask which material for which tooth, and why. A clinic that answers tooth by tooth is planning. A clinic that answers with one material for the whole mouth is quoting.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zirconium-crown/">Zirconium Crown</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/e-max-lumineers/">E Max Lumineers</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/onlay-inlay/">Onlay Inlay</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'dhi-vs-sapphire-fue',
			'title' => 'DHI or Sapphire FUE: an honest comparison',
			'excerpt' => 'Two techniques marketed as though one is newer and better. What genuinely differs, what does not, and which cases actually favour each.',
			'dek' => 'Most of the difference is in how the graft gets into the skin, not in what happens after.',
			'cat' => 'Choosing a treatment',
			'read' => 12,
			'cover' => 'blog-dhi-vs-sapphire-fue',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Both are FUE. They differ in one step: DHI creates and fills the site in a single motion, sapphire FUE opens sites first.', 'DHI suits hairlines, unshaven cases and placing among existing hair. Sapphire FUE suits large areas and more grafts per session.', 'Time out of body, who holds the instruments, and the angles at the hairline predict the result far better than the technique name.'),
			'content' => '<p>Both are follicular unit extraction. Both take grafts one at a time from the back of the head. The marketing presents them as different generations of technology; in practice they differ in one step, and that step has real consequences for particular cases.</p><h2 id="what-actually-differs">What actually differs</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Sapphire FUE</th><th scope="col">DHI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Incisions</th><td>Made first, with a sapphire blade, then grafts placed into them</td><td>Made and filled in one motion by an implanter pen</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Instrument</th><td>Crystalline sapphire blade</td><td>Choi implanter pen</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Shaving</th><td>Recipient area usually shaved</td><td>Can often be done without shaving the recipient area</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Speed</th><td>Faster per graft</td><td>Slower — more time per graft</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Density control</th><td>Very good; sites planned as a set</td><td>Excellent; each graft placed individually</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Best suited to</th><td>Larger areas, full crown coverage</td><td>Hairlines, filling among existing hair, unshaven cases</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="the-sapphire-blade-honestly">The sapphire blade, honestly</h2><p>Sapphire blades are sharper and hold an edge better than steel, and they make a slightly different channel shape. The claimed benefits — less tissue trauma, faster healing, tighter placement — are plausible and modest. What they are not is a different operation. A skilled surgeon with steel blades produces better results than an unskilled one with sapphire.</p><h2 id="the-implanter-pen-honestly">The implanter pen, honestly</h2><p>DHI’s real advantage is that the graft spends less time out of the body and goes in without a second handling step, and that placing among existing hair is easier because you are not opening blind channels between follicles you cannot see. That makes it genuinely better for two situations: a hairline where every angle matters, and a thinning crown with hair still in it.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>It is slower, which is why some clinics quote fewer grafts for DHI, or quote a higher price, or quietly do a hybrid — DHI at the hairline and FUE behind it. The hybrid is often the right answer clinically. Ask which you are actually getting, area by area.</p></aside><h2 id="what-matters-more-than-either">What matters more than either</h2><ul><li>Who is holding the instruments, and for how many hours</li><li>How long grafts spend outside the body, and in what</li><li>Whether the angles at the hairline follow your existing growth</li><li>Whether the density was planned for how it looks grown out, not on day one</li><li>Whether anyone has told you your native hair will keep receding</li></ul><p>Those five predict the result far better than the choice of blade. A clinic that talks about technique names and not about any of the five is selling a brand.</p><h2 id="time-out-of-body-and-why-it-is-the-numbe">Time out of body, and why it is the number that matters</h2><p>Once a follicular unit leaves your scalp it is a piece of tissue without a blood supply, and its survival falls the longer it stays that way. Everything that separates a good result from a mediocre one runs through this single variable — and it is barely mentioned in the marketing, because it is about process rather than instruments.</p><ul><li>How grafts are stored between extraction and placement, and at what temperature</li><li>How many people are placing, and therefore how fast the batch clears</li><li>Whether extraction and placement overlap, or run one after the other</li><li>How long the whole session takes for your graft number</li></ul><p>DHI’s real technical advantage is here rather than in the pen itself: because the site is created and filled in one motion, the graft goes from holding solution into skin without an intermediate handling step. Whether that advantage survives a clinic that keeps you on the table for nine hours is another matter.</p><h2 id="the-unshaven-question">The unshaven question</h2><p>DHI is frequently marketed as the unshaven technique. It is more accurate to say that unshaven work is easier with an implanter pen, and that unshaven is a spectrum: fully unshaven, a shaved window at the back hidden under longer hair, or a shaved recipient area with the donor left long. The middle option is the commonest and is available with either technique.</p><p>Unshaven work is slower and yields fewer grafts per session. If discretion matters more to you than maximum coverage in one go, that is a perfectly rational trade — just make sure it is a trade you chose rather than one you discovered afterwards.</p><h2 id="cost-and-why-dhi-is-usually-dearer">Cost, and why DHI is usually dearer</h2><p>Implanter pens are consumables and the technique takes longer per graft, so clinics either charge more or quote fewer grafts. Neither is a swindle. What you should refuse is a DHI price with an FUE graft count, or a package where “DHI” turns out to mean the hairline only while the rest is standard FUE — unless that hybrid is what you agreed, which is often the best clinical answer anyway.</p><h2 id="which-to-choose-plainly">Which to choose, plainly</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">If your case is…</th><th scope="col">The technique that usually suits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">A large area, mostly bald, maximum coverage in one session</th><td>Sapphire FUE</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A hairline where every angle will be scrutinised</th><td>DHI, or a hybrid with DHI at the front</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thinning with native hair still present</th><td>DHI — placing among existing hair without damaging it</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You do not want a visible shave</th><td>DHI, unshaven or with a hidden donor window</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Budget is the binding constraint</th><td>Sapphire FUE, and put the difference into surgeon time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>And in every case, the five things in the previous section matter more than the answer in this table. A hybrid plan from a surgeon who has examined your donor area beats the right technique name from someone who has seen three photographs.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/dhi-hair-transplant/">Dhi Hair Transplant</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/manual-fue/">Manual Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'how-long-in-istanbul-dental-treatment',
			'title' => 'How long you actually need in Istanbul, by treatment',
			'excerpt' => 'Realistic day counts for each treatment, why almost everything needs two trips, and what the clinic means when it says “one week”.',
			'dek' => 'The number of days is a clinical constraint, not a package option.',
			'cat' => 'Planning your trip',
			'read' => 7,
			'cover' => 'blog-how-long-in-istanbul-dental-treatment',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Crowns and veneers: five to seven days, one trip. Anything involving an implant: two trips, three to six months apart.', 'Osseointegration takes three to six months and cannot be shortened. Same-day teeth are a temporary, by design.', 'Add two buffer days and never schedule an appointment on the day you fly home.'),
			'content' => '<p>Book flights after the plan, never before. The number of days is set by how long ceramic takes to make and how long bone takes to heal, and neither of those negotiates.</p><h2 id="the-realistic-ranges">The realistic ranges</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Treatment</th><th scope="col">Trip one</th><th scope="col">Trip two</th><th scope="col">Gap between</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Crowns or veneers</th><td>5–7 days</td><td>Usually none</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Single implant</th><td>3–4 days</td><td>5–7 days</td><td>3–6 months</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Implant with immediate crown</th><td>5–7 days</td><td>5–7 days</td><td>3–6 months</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Full arch (All-on-4 / All-on-6)</th><td>7–10 days</td><td>7–10 days</td><td>3–6 months</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bone graft or sinus lift</th><td>3–5 days</td><td>Implants later, separately</td><td>4–9 months</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hair transplant</th><td>3–4 days</td><td>None</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class="post-note"><p>Everything involving an implant is two trips. A clinic offering a permanent implant-borne tooth in a single week has either given you a temporary and called it final, or is loading a fixture that has not integrated. Ask which.</p></aside><h2 id="why-crowns-take-a-week-and-not-two-days">Why crowns take a week and not two days</h2><p>A crown is prepared, scanned, milled, layered, stained, glazed, tried in, adjusted and cemented. The try-in is the point — it is where the shade and the bite get corrected while correction is still easy. Clinics that finish in two days have skipped it, and the fastest way to end up with a crown that is the wrong shade is to compress the week into a long weekend.</p><h2 id="why-implants-take-months">Why implants take months</h2><p>Osseointegration — bone growing onto the fixture surface — takes three to six months and cannot be accelerated. Immediate loading protocols put a temporary tooth on straight away so you are never without teeth, but the temporary is a temporary. The definitive prosthesis is fitted on the second trip, after integration, and that is not a scheduling preference.</p><h2 id="building-the-itinerary">Building the itinerary</h2><ul><li>Add two buffer days at the end. Swelling, a laboratory remake, or a bite that needs another adjustment are all normal and all cost a day.</li><li>Do not schedule anything on the day you fly home.</li><li>For sedation cases, bring somebody. You cannot be discharged alone.</li><li>Keep the last afternoon free for the final check rather than for the Grand Bazaar.</li></ul><h2 id="the-question-to-ask">The question to ask</h2><p>Ask for the day-by-day schedule before you book anything: what happens on each day, and which day is the last clinical appointment. A clinic that can send that immediately has done it many times. A clinic that says “about a week” has not thought about your case yet.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zirconium-crown/">Zirconium Crown</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'flying-after-dental-surgery',
			'title' => 'When you can fly after dental surgery or a hair transplant',
			'excerpt' => 'What cabin pressure does to a healing site, which procedures need a delay, and the one situation where flying too early causes real damage.',
			'dek' => 'For most procedures the answer is “the next day”. For two of them it is not.',
			'cat' => 'Planning your trip',
			'read' => 6,
			'cover' => 'blog-flying-after-dental-surgery',
			'credit' => 'Free airport departure board image —  (CC0 1.0), via rawpixel',
			'credit_url' => 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/5917082/image-light-public-domain-free',
			'keys' => array('Most dental work: fly the next day. Surgical extractions: 48 to 72 hours.', 'After a sinus lift, wait two to four weeks. This is the one case where flying early can displace the graft and cost you the procedure.', 'After a hair transplant you can fly at 24 to 48 hours, but nothing may touch the grafts — aisle seat, no overhead locker, sleep upright.'),
			'content' => '<p>Cabins are pressurised to the equivalent of six to eight thousand feet. That drop in pressure makes trapped gas expand, and it dries every mucous membrane you have. For most dental work neither matters. For sinus work, both do.</p><h2 id="the-rules-of-thumb">The rules of thumb</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Procedure</th><th scope="col">Usual guidance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Crowns, veneers, cleaning</th><td>Same day or next day</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Simple extraction</th><td>Next day, usually fine</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Implant placement, no sinus involvement</th><td>24–48 hours</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Surgical extraction, wisdom teeth</th><td>48–72 hours</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sinus lift</th><td>2–4 weeks, and this one is not flexible</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Hair transplant</th><td>24–48 hours, with precautions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class="post-note"><p>After a sinus lift the membrane above your upper back teeth has been deliberately raised and grafted. Pressure change acts directly on it, and a sneeze or a pressurised descent too early can displace the graft. This is the one case where flying early can cost you the procedure. Follow the surgeon’s date, not the airline’s change fee.</p></aside><h2 id="flying-after-a-hair-transplant">Flying after a hair transplant</h2><p>Physically you can fly the day after. The problem is not the flight, it is everything around it — overhead lockers, headrests, sleeping against a seat back, and other people’s bags. The grafts are not secure for about ten days and anything that rubs them can dislodge one permanently.</p><ul><li>Take an aisle seat, and do not put anything in the overhead locker yourself</li><li>Wear the loose hat the clinic gives you; nothing tight, nothing pulled on over the recipient area</li><li>Do not sleep against the seat back — bring a neck pillow and stay upright</li><li>Expect swelling to move down to the forehead and around the eyes on days two to four. It is normal and it resolves.</li></ul><h2 id="practical-points-for-any-post-surgical-f">Practical points for any post-surgical flight</h2><ul><li>Carry your medication in hand luggage with the prescription</li><li>Cabin air is dry — drink water, avoid alcohol entirely for the first few days</li><li>Take a cold pack through security in an empty bag and fill it after</li><li>Carry your treatment record and X-rays, in case anyone needs to see them en route</li></ul><h2 id="insurance">Insurance</h2><p>Standard travel policies exclude elective treatment abroad and its complications. If you need to change flights because a surgeon has told you not to fly, a normal policy will not cover it. A specific medical-travel policy, bought before departure, might. Read the exclusions rather than the headline.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sinus-lift/">Sinus Lift</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/bone-graft/">Bone Graft</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'first-14-days-after-hair-transplant',
			'title' => 'The first fourteen days after a hair transplant',
			'excerpt' => 'Day by day: what is normal, what is not, and the two things people do in week one that cost them grafts.',
			'dek' => 'Almost everything alarming in the first fortnight is supposed to happen.',
			'cat' => 'Recovery and aftercare',
			'read' => 8,
			'cover' => 'blog-first-14-days-after-hair-transplant',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Swelling peaks around day three and travels down to the forehead and eyes. It is normal and it resolves.', 'The two mistakes that cost grafts are picking the scabs and returning to the gym before day ten.', 'Between weeks two and six most transplanted hairs shed. That is the follicle resetting, not the graft failing.'),
			'content' => '<p>The first two weeks look worse than they are, and the anxiety is mostly caused by nobody having described in advance what normal looks like. Here it is.</p><h2 id="days-1-to-3">Days 1 to 3</h2><p>The recipient area is covered in small scabs, one per graft, and the donor area feels tight and sore. There may be some oozing on the first night — sleep on a towel, at about forty-five degrees. Swelling usually begins on day two and travels downward: forehead first, then around the eyes. It can be dramatic on day three and it is not a complication.</p><ul><li>Sleep elevated, on your back, for the first week</li><li>Nothing touches the recipient area — no hats that pull on, no pillows against it</li><li>Cold compress on the forehead only, never on the grafts</li><li>No alcohol, no smoking, no exercise</li></ul><h2 id="days-4-to-10">Days 4 to 10</h2><p>Swelling resolves. Washing begins as the clinic showed you: lotion on, left to soften, then rinsed with a cup rather than a shower head. The point is to soften the crusts so they release on their own. The point is not to remove them.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>The two mistakes that cost grafts, both in this window: picking the scabs, and going back to the gym. A graft is not secure until around day ten, and once one is pulled out it does not go back in. Everything else you can get wrong is recoverable.</p></aside><h2 id="days-10-to-14">Days 10 to 14</h2><p>The crusts have gone and the transplanted hairs are visible, short and fine. The donor area has settled to pink dots that are already hard to see. This is the point at which most people look normal to anyone not inspecting closely.</p><h2 id="then-it-falls-out-and-that-is-fine">Then it falls out, and that is fine</h2><p>Between weeks two and six, most of the transplanted hairs shed. This is shock loss, it happens to nearly everyone, and it is not the grafts failing — the follicle stays in the skin and re-enters its growth cycle. It is the single most alarming stage and the single most normal one.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">What you see</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Weeks 2–6</th><td>Transplanted hairs shed. The area looks as it did before.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 3–4</th><td>New growth begins, fine and often lighter than your own</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 6–9</th><td>Real density appears; texture starts to normalise</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 12–18</th><td>Final result. This is when you judge it, not before.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="when-to-actually-call-somebody">When to actually call somebody</h2><ul><li>Fever, or spreading redness and heat around the donor or recipient area</li><li>Pus, as distinct from the normal crusting</li><li>Pain that increases after day three rather than decreasing</li><li>Bleeding that does not stop with ten minutes of gentle pressure</li></ul><p>None of these are common. All of them are worth a message the same day, and any clinic worth travelling to will answer one.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/dhi-hair-transplant/">Dhi Hair Transplant</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/manual-fue/">Manual Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'how-many-grafts-do-i-need',
			'title' => 'How many grafts do you actually need?',
			'excerpt' => 'Where the number comes from, why the Norwood scale is only the starting point, and why the answer depends more on your donor area than on your bald patch.',
			'dek' => 'The limit is not what you want covered. It is what the back of your head can spare.',
			'cat' => 'Choosing a treatment',
			'read' => 7,
			'cover' => 'blog-how-many-grafts-do-i-need',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('The ceiling is set by your donor area, not by the area you want covered. A typical donor holds 5,000 to 7,000 grafts for a lifetime.', 'A real number comes from measured donor density and hair calibre, plus a check for miniaturisation in the donor zone itself.', 'Native hair keeps receding after a transplant. A surgeon planning properly designs conservatively and holds grafts in reserve.'),
			'content' => '<p>Everyone asks about the area they want covered. The number is actually set at the other end of the head, by how much the donor area can give up without ever looking harvested — because that supply is finite, does not regenerate, and has to last the rest of your life.</p><h2 id="the-rough-numbers">The rough numbers</h2><p>These are typical ranges for a first procedure. Yours will be set by measurement, not by this table.</p><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">What is being covered</th><th scope="col">Typical graft range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Hairline lowering or refinement only</th><td>1,000–2,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Receding temples and front third</th><td>2,000–3,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Front and mid-scalp</th><td>3,000–4,500</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Crown only</th><td>1,500–2,500</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Beard or moustache</th><td>1,000–3,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Eyebrows (both)</th><td>300–700</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="why-the-donor-area-sets-the-ceiling">Why the donor area sets the ceiling</h2><p>The safe donor zone is a band around the back and sides where follicles are genetically resistant to the hormone driving male pattern loss. A typical donor holds somewhere around 5,000 to 7,000 extractable grafts over a lifetime, across all procedures you will ever have. Take too many at once and the donor thins visibly — and that is permanent, obvious in short haircuts, and very hard to repair.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>This is why “unlimited grafts” is the phrase to walk away from. A clinic that treats your donor area as unlimited is spending an asset you will need again in ten years, when your native hair has receded further and you want a second procedure.</p></aside><h2 id="what-a-real-assessment-involves">What a real assessment involves</h2><ul><li>Donor density measured with a densitometer, not estimated from a photo</li><li>Hair calibre — thicker hair covers more scalp per graft, so fewer grafts go further</li><li>Checking the donor zone itself for miniaturisation, which changes everything</li><li>Your Norwood stage, and a judgement about where it is heading</li><li>Whether you are on medication that may hold your native hair</li></ul><h2 id="the-part-clinics-skip">The part clinics skip</h2><p>Androgenetic loss continues after a transplant. If a hairline is built at twenty-five and nothing is done about the native hair behind it, that hair keeps receding and you are left with a transplanted island and a gap behind it. A surgeon planning properly will design conservatively, discuss whether medication is appropriate for you, and hold grafts in reserve.</p><p>A clinic that offers you the most aggressive hairline you ask for, with the largest graft number, in one session, has optimised for the sale rather than for how you will look at forty.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/dhi-hair-transplant/">Dhi Hair Transplant</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/female-hair-transplant/">Female Hair Transplant</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'looking-after-dental-implants',
			'title' => 'Making dental implants last twenty years',
			'excerpt' => 'Implants do not decay, which is exactly why people neglect them. What actually causes late failure, and the routine that prevents it.',
			'dek' => 'The implant is titanium. The gum around it is not, and that is where implants are lost.',
			'cat' => 'Recovery and aftercare',
			'read' => 6,
			'cover' => 'blog-looking-after-dental-implants',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Titanium cannot decay. Implants are lost to peri-implantitis — inflammation of the gum and bone around them — which is painless until it is advanced.', 'That is why implants need more checking than natural teeth, not less: there is no toothache to warn you.', 'Smoking is the largest modifiable risk. Grinding is next, and the night guard is not optional.'),
			'content' => '<p>The good news is that a well-placed implant in a well-maintained mouth has a very high long-term survival rate. The bad news is that “well-maintained” does a lot of work in that sentence, and the failure mode is one most people have never heard of.</p><h2 id="peri-implantitis-which-is-the-thing-to-k">Peri-implantitis, which is the thing to know about</h2><p>Titanium cannot decay. What can happen is inflammation of the gum and bone around the implant — peri-implantitis — driven by plaque at the margin, exactly like gum disease around a natural tooth. It is usually painless until it is advanced, and by then the bone that has been lost does not come back.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>This is why implants need checking more, not less, than natural teeth. There is no toothache to warn you. The only early warning is a hygienist noticing bleeding and a deepening pocket at a routine appointment.</p></aside><h2 id="the-routine">The routine</h2><ul><li>Brush twice daily, with attention to the margin where crown meets gum</li><li>Clean between them every day — interdental brushes sized by your hygienist, not guessed at</li><li>A water flosser is a useful addition around bridges and full arches; it is not a replacement for brushing</li><li>Professional cleaning every six months, with instruments appropriate for implants</li><li>X-rays periodically, to check bone levels against the baseline from placement</li></ul><h2 id="the-four-things-that-shorten-an-implant-">The four things that shorten an implant’s life</h2><ul><li>Smoking. It is the single largest modifiable risk factor, and the effect is large.</li><li>Uncontrolled diabetes, which impairs the healing the implant depends on.</li><li>Grinding, which loads the fixture in ways it was not designed for. If you grind, wear the guard.</li><li>Skipping hygiene appointments, because peri-implantitis is silent and only found by looking.</li></ul><h2 id="what-to-bring-home-from-istanbul">What to bring home from Istanbul</h2><p>Ask for all of it before you leave: the implant passport naming the fixture brand, model and size; your post-placement X-rays; the treatment record; and the warranty document. Any dentist anywhere in the world can help you far more easily with those in hand — and without them, a component that needs replacing in twelve years becomes a much harder problem.</p><h2 id="find-a-dentist-at-home-before-you-need-o">Find a dentist at home before you need one</h2><p>Arrange a check-up with a local dentist within a few months of finishing treatment, while everything is fine. It establishes a baseline with somebody who can see you at short notice, and it means the first time they meet your implants is not the day something has gone wrong.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/night-guard/">Night Guard</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'what-to-pack-treatment-trip-istanbul',
			'title' => 'What to pack for a treatment trip to Istanbul',
			'excerpt' => 'A practical list, including the four documents that matter more than anything in your suitcase and the things nobody thinks of until day two.',
			'dek' => 'Most of this is boring. The document list is not.',
			'cat' => 'Planning your trip',
			'read' => 5,
			'cover' => 'blog-what-to-pack-treatment-trip-istanbul',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('The four documents matter more than anything in the suitcase: printed plan, medication list with generic names, scans on a USB stick, insurance details.', 'For a hair transplant, pack only button-up tops — nothing goes over your head for ten days.', 'Turkey uses the Type F two-pin plug, and everything with the clinic is coordinated on WhatsApp.'),
			'content' => '<p>Packing for a treatment trip is packing for a short holiday plus four documents and a handful of things that make the recovery days bearable.</p><h2 id="the-documents-which-matter-most">The documents, which matter most</h2><ul><li>Your written treatment plan and quote, printed. Not a screenshot in a chat thread.</li><li>A list of every medication you take, with doses, and any allergies. Generic names, not brand names — brands differ between countries.</li><li>Any recent X-rays or CBCT scans on a USB stick as well as by email.</li><li>Travel insurance details, and the number to call on them.</li></ul><aside class="post-note"><p>Take a photograph of your passport, your plan and your insurance and email it to yourself. If your phone goes missing on day one, that copy is what saves the week.</p></aside><h2 id="for-dental-treatment">For dental treatment</h2><ul><li>Soft food you actually like. Hotel breakfasts are bread. Bring what you would want to eat when chewing is unappealing.</li><li>A travel pillow — useful for propping yourself up in bed, not just on the plane.</li><li>Lip balm. Retractors dry your lips out badly over a long appointment.</li><li>Any nightguard or retainer you already wear.</li><li>Paracetamol you recognise, even though the clinic will supply medication.</li></ul><h2 id="for-a-hair-transplant">For a hair transplant</h2><ul><li>Button-up or zip tops only. Nothing you have to pull over your head, for at least ten days.</li><li>A loose hat for the flight home — the clinic usually provides one, but bring a spare that does not grip.</li><li>A neck pillow, so you can sleep upright without touching the back of your head to anything.</li><li>A dark towel for the first night or two.</li></ul><h2 id="practical-istanbul">Practical Istanbul</h2><ul><li>A Type F plug adapter — Turkey uses the European two-pin.</li><li>Some Turkish lira for taxis and small purchases; cards work almost everywhere else.</li><li>An eSIM or a roaming plan, because everything is coordinated on WhatsApp.</li><li>Clothing for the weather you are actually getting. Istanbul is cold and wet from December to March and genuinely hot in July and August.</li><li>Comfortable shoes, and modest cover for shoulders and knees if you plan to visit mosques.</li></ul><h2 id="what-to-leave-at-home">What to leave at home</h2><p>Ambitious plans. People book a treatment week and a sightseeing week in the same five days and then spend day three swollen in a hotel room feeling they are wasting the trip. Plan the treatment, and treat anything else as a bonus if you feel like it.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'realistic-timeline-before-after',
			'title' => 'What a realistic before-and-after timeline looks like',
			'excerpt' => 'Galleries show day one and month twelve. Everything difficult happens in between. What each stage actually looks like, for teeth and for hair.',
			'dek' => 'The gap between the two photographs is where people panic, and nobody photographs it.',
			'cat' => 'Recovery and aftercare',
			'read' => 7,
			'cover' => 'blog-realistic-timeline-before-after',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('For hair, month three is when people conclude it has failed. It has not — judge nothing before month six and nothing finally before twelve.', 'For a full arch, months two to four are integration: nothing visible changes, and that part cannot be hurried.', 'When reading a gallery, check the dates, the lighting and the angle, and ask whether the photographs are that clinic’s own patients.'),
			'content' => '<p>Every clinic gallery is two photographs. The distance between them is months, and most of those months look nothing like either picture. Knowing the shape of it in advance is the difference between a normal recovery and a frightening one.</p><h2 id="a-hair-transplant-month-by-month">A hair transplant, month by month</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">What it actually looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Days 1–5</th><td>Scabbed, swollen, obviously operated on</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Days 10–14</th><td>Crusts gone; short transplanted hairs visible</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Weeks 2–6</th><td>Shedding. It looks as it did before the procedure. This is normal.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 3–4</th><td>First new growth — fine, sparse, often lighter</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 6–9</th><td>Real density; texture beginning to normalise</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 12–18</th><td>The result. This is the photograph in the gallery.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class="post-note"><p>Month three is when people conclude it has failed. It has not. The shed is the follicle resetting its cycle, and growth after it is the normal course. Do not judge anything before month six, and do not judge it finally before month twelve.</p></aside><h2 id="a-full-arch-implant-case">A full-arch implant case</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">What is happening</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Day of surgery</th><td>Fixed temporary teeth fitted the same day in most protocols</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Weeks 1–2</th><td>Swelling and bruising resolve; soft diet throughout</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 2–4</th><td>Integration. Nothing visible changes; this is the part that cannot be rushed.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Second trip</th><td>Definitive prosthesis fitted after tissues have settled</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months 6–12</th><td>Bite refined; you stop noticing them</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="crowns-and-veneers">Crowns and veneers</h2><p>Much shorter, and the thing to expect is different: sensitivity for a week or two, and a settling-in period where the bite gets adjusted. If a new crown feels high, it needs adjusting — it will not wear in, and leaving it stresses the tooth beneath.</p><h2 id="how-to-read-a-gallery-honestly">How to read a gallery honestly</h2><ul><li>Look for dates on both photographs, and check the gap is at least twelve months for hair</li><li>Look for the same lighting and the same angle. Different lighting is the oldest trick there is.</li><li>Look for wet hair in the "before" and dry styled hair in the "after"</li><li>Ask for a case like yours — same starting point, same age, same hair type</li><li>Ask whether the photographs are the clinic’s own patients. Frequently they are not.</li></ul><h2 id="set-the-expectation-properly">Set the expectation properly</h2><p>A transplant redistributes a finite supply of hair; it does not create more. A full arch restores function and appearance; it is not the dentition you had at twenty. Clinics that promise otherwise get the deposit and then spend a year managing a disappointed patient. The ones worth travelling to tell you what is achievable at the assessment, in writing, before you book anything.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zirconium-crown/">Zirconium Crown</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'do-i-need-bone-graft-or-sinus-lift',
			'title' => 'Do you need a bone graft or a sinus lift?',
			'excerpt' => 'Why a third of upper implant cases need extra work, how it is decided, and what it does to your timeline and your budget.',
			'dek' => 'This is the item most likely to change your quote after you arrive — unless it was scanned for first.',
			'cat' => 'Choosing a treatment',
			'read' => 7,
			'cover' => 'blog-do-i-need-bone-graft-or-sinus-lift',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Only a CBCT scan can answer this. A quote that does not mention grafting either way was not made by anyone looking at your bone.', 'The consequence is time more than money — a lateral window sinus lift can add four to nine months and a third trip.', 'Tilted implants, shorter fixtures or zygomatic implants avoid grafting in some cases. Which applies is a judgement from your scan.'),
			'content' => '<p>An implant needs bone to hold it. When a tooth has been missing for a while, the bone that used to support it resorbs — it is no longer being loaded, so the body reclaims it. In the upper back jaw the sinus also expands downward into the space. Both leave less height than an implant requires.</p><h2 id="how-it-is-decided">How it is decided</h2><p>By a CBCT scan and nothing else. The scan measures the height and width of bone at each planned site in three dimensions. A panoramic X-ray is not enough, and photographs are not remotely enough. This is the single strongest reason not to accept a fixed implant quote from a clinic that has not scanned you.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>If your quote does not mention grafting either way, it was not made by somebody looking at your bone. The commonest unpleasant surprise in dental tourism is arriving for implants and being told on day one that a graft is needed, at extra cost and with a delay of several months.</p></aside><h2 id="the-options-roughly-in-order-of-size">The options, roughly in order of size</h2><div class="post-table"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Procedure</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">Effect on timeline</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Socket preservation</th><td>Graft placed at the time of extraction to limit resorption</td><td>Usually none — it prevents the problem</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Guided bone regeneration</th><td>Graft material and a membrane to rebuild width or height</td><td>4–9 months before implants</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sinus lift (crestal)</th><td>Membrane raised a small amount through the implant site</td><td>Often same visit as the implant</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sinus lift (lateral window)</th><td>Larger lift through a side window, for greater height</td><td>4–9 months before implants</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Zygomatic implants</th><td>Anchored in the cheekbone, avoiding grafting entirely</td><td>Avoids the wait; a bigger procedure</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="what-it-means-for-your-trip">What it means for your trip</h2><p>The important consequence is not cost, it is time. A lateral window sinus lift means one trip for the graft, several months of healing, and a separate trip for the implants — so a treatment you imagined as two visits becomes three, spread over a year or more.</p><p>Crestal lifts and socket preservation frequently add no time at all. That is why the scan matters: the difference between “no delay” and “nine months” is a measurement.</p><h2 id="can-it-be-avoided">Can it be avoided?</h2><p>Sometimes. Shorter or narrower implants can work where height is limited. Tilted implants, as in the All-on-4 protocol, are specifically designed to engage bone in front of the sinus and often avoid grafting for a full arch. Zygomatic implants avoid it in severe cases. Which of those is appropriate is a judgement from your scan, and if a clinic offers you one without having looked at it, that is a sales answer.</p><h2 id="the-question-to-ask-before-you-pay-a-dep">The question to ask before you pay a deposit</h2><p>“Based on my CBCT, will I need grafting — and if the answer changes on the day, what will it cost and how will it change my dates?” Get it in writing. A clinic that answers precisely has planned your case. A clinic that says it will be fine has not looked.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/bone-graft/">Bone Graft</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sinus-lift/">Sinus Lift</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zygomatic-implants/">Zygomatic Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'dental-tourism-what-if-it-goes-wrong',
			'title' => 'What happens if something goes wrong after you fly home',
			'excerpt' => 'The question that decides whether treatment abroad was a good idea. What warranties actually cover, what your options are at home, and how to set this up before you travel.',
			'dek' => 'Nearly every complication happens after you have left the country. Plan for that, not for the surgery.',
			'cat' => 'Safety and choosing a clinic',
			'read' => 8,
			'cover' => 'blog-dental-tourism-what-if-it-goes-wrong',
			'credit' => '',
			'credit_url' => '',
			'keys' => array('Nearly every complication happens after you fly home, and that is the part almost nobody plans for.', 'Most warranties cover the component, not your flights or a local dentist’s fee. Read the document, not the sentence in the chat.', 'Leave Istanbul with your implant passport, X-rays and treatment record. They are what let any dentist anywhere help you.'),
			'content' => '<p>This is the part of dental tourism that people think about least and that determines their experience most. The surgery is a week. The next twenty years are where things either hold up or do not, and you will be two thousand miles away for all of it.</p><h2 id="what-usually-goes-wrong-and-when">What usually goes wrong, and when</h2><ul><li>A crown debonds. Common, minor, fixable by any dentist in an appointment.</li><li>A screw loosens under a bridge or an arch. Needs the right driver for that system — which is why the fixture brand matters.</li><li>A bite that was never quite right. Should have been caught at the fitting; becomes a problem months later.</li><li>Peri-implantitis. Slow, painless, and only found by someone checking.</li><li>An implant fails to integrate. Uncommon, and usually apparent within the first few months.</li></ul><p>Almost all of those are manageable. What makes them expensive is not having decided in advance who deals with them.</p><h2 id="what-a-warranty-actually-covers">What a warranty actually covers</h2><p>Read the document, not the sentence in the chat. Most clinic warranties cover the component: they will replace a failed implant or a fractured crown. What they almost never cover is your flights, your hotel, or a local dentist’s fee for doing the work in your own country.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>A “lifetime guarantee” that requires you to fly to Istanbul to claim it is a guarantee on a component, not a guarantee of care. That may still be perfectly good value — but you should know which one you have bought.</p></aside><h2 id="questions-to-settle-before-you-travel">Questions to settle before you travel</h2><ul><li>What is covered, for how long, and what voids it? (Smoking and missed hygiene visits void many.)</li><li>If a crown debonds at home, do you contribute to a local dentist’s fee?</li><li>Do you have any partner dentists in my country?</li><li>Who do I contact, on what number, and what is the response time?</li><li>Will I leave with my implant passport, X-rays and full treatment record?</li></ul><h2 id="set-up-your-side-too">Set up your side too</h2><p>Two things, both worth doing before you go. Find a dentist at home who is willing to see you for maintenance and knows you have had work abroad — some are reluctant, and it is much better to discover that now than in an emergency. And keep your records: passport, X-rays, plan, warranty, all in one folder, digital and paper.</p><h2 id="the-legal-position-honestly">The legal position, honestly</h2><p>Pursuing a complaint against a clinic in another jurisdiction is slow, conducted in Turkish, and rarely proportionate to the sums involved. Turkey does have a real complaints route through the provincial health directorate and the Ministry’s health tourism channels, and it is worth knowing it exists. It is not worth relying on.</p><p>Which is the real argument for the boring checks before you travel. The recourse that works is choosing a clinic that answers awkward questions clearly, in writing, while it is still trying to win your business.</p><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/zirconium-crown/">Zirconium Crown</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/full-mouth-dental-implants/">Full Mouth Dental Implants</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
		array(
			'slug' => 'istanbul-for-patients-practical-guide',
			'title' => 'Istanbul for patients: a practical guide',
			'excerpt' => 'Where to stay relative to the clinics, how to get around when you would rather not, and what is actually worth doing on the days you feel well.',
			'dek' => 'You are here for a week and you will spend most of it not in a clinic.',
			'cat' => 'Planning your trip',
			'read' => 6,
			'cover' => 'blog-istanbul-for-patients-practical-guide',
			'credit' => 'Contemplando el Bósforo — Carlos ZGZ (CC0 1.0), via flickr',
			'credit_url' => 'https://www.flickr.com/photos/31810462@N07/20556261771',
			'keys' => array('Stay near the clinic, not near the sights. Most clinics are in Şişli, Nişantaşı, Levent or Etiler; Sultanahmet is 45 minutes of traffic away.', 'Confirm which airport your transfer is for — Istanbul Airport and Sabiha Gökçen are on opposite sides of the city.', 'Skip the hammam while healing: heat and steam are exactly what you have been told to avoid.'),
			'content' => '<p>Istanbul is a city of sixteen million people across two continents, and the difference between a comfortable treatment week and an exhausting one is mostly geography.</p><h2 id="where-to-stay">Where to stay</h2><p>Stay near the clinic, not near the sights. Most dental and hair clinics are on the European side in Şişli, Nişantaşı, Levent or Etiler — business districts, well connected, quiet in the evening. Sultanahmet, where the Blue Mosque and Hagia Sophia are, is beautiful and is forty-five minutes of traffic away from the clinics. On a day when your face is swollen, that distance is the only thing you will notice.</p><ul><li>Ask the clinic for its address and book within a fifteen-minute drive</li><li>Nişantaşı and Şişli: central, walkable, plenty of pharmacies and quiet restaurants</li><li>Levent and Etiler: newer hotels, calmer, slightly further out</li><li>A room with a kettle and a fridge is worth more than a view when you are on soft food</li></ul><h2 id="getting-around">Getting around</h2><ul><li>Most clinics include airport transfers. Confirm which airport — Istanbul Airport (IST) and Sabiha Gökçen (SAW) are on opposite sides of the city and an hour and a half apart.</li><li>Use a ride-hailing app rather than hailing taxis; it removes the fare conversation entirely.</li><li>The M2 metro line runs through Şişli and Levent and is faster than a car at rush hour.</li><li>Traffic between about 07:30 and 10:00 and again from 17:00 is genuinely severe. Leave early for appointments.</li></ul><h2 id="eating-when-you-are-on-soft-food">Eating when you are on soft food</h2><p>Istanbul is unexpectedly good at this. Mercimek çorbası, the lentil soup available everywhere, is warm, cheap and requires no chewing. Yoghurt, sütlaç, and menemen are all easy. Avoid anything hot in temperature for the first days after surgery, and avoid anything with small seeds after extractions.</p><h2 id="practical-things">Practical things</h2><ul><li>Pharmacies (eczane) are everywhere and pharmacists frequently speak some English</li><li>Tap water is chlorinated but most people drink bottled</li><li>Cards are accepted almost everywhere; keep some lira for taxis and markets</li><li>Turkey uses the European two-pin plug, Type F</li></ul><h2 id="what-to-do-on-the-good-days">What to do on the good days</h2><p>Keep it low-effort. A ferry across the Bosphorus is an hour, costs almost nothing, involves no walking and is the best thing in the city. Sitting in a tea garden in Ortaköy or Bebek is a legitimate afternoon. The Basilica Cistern is cool, flat and close together.</p><aside class="post-note"><p>Two things to skip while healing: the hammam, because heat and steam are exactly what you have been told to avoid after surgery, and any long day on your feet in the Grand Bazaar. Both will still be there when you come back for the second trip.</p></aside><aside class="post-related"><p class="post-related__k">Read next</p><ul><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/single-dental-implants/">Single Dental Implants</a></li><li><a href="%VH_HOME%/services/sapphire-fue/">Sapphire Fue</a></li></ul></aside>',
		),
	);
}
